00:16:51.236 --> 00:16:54.276
So how do we work with NIE to achieve those?

00:16:55.156 --> 00:17:02.476
Now, one of the key tools we use for NIE
modernization journey through AIDLC was AWS Transform custom.

00:17:02.796 --> 00:17:10.956
AWS Transform custom is a command line, interface based autonomous
agent that supports virtually any code transformation scenarios.

00:17:11.236 --> 00:17:18.196
It targets specific frameworks, language to language conversion,
version upgrades, and even architectural changes.

00:17:18.596 --> 00:17:23.476
AWS Transform custom breaks down the barrier
of language and framework lock in,

00:17:23.516 --> 00:17:26.756
putting the power of customization directly into your hands.

00:17:27.076 --> 00:17:30.156
And one of its key differentiator is continual learning.

00:17:30.676 --> 00:17:37.596
The AWS Transform Custom Agent improves with every run,
incorporating feedback to become smarter over time.

00:17:38.076 --> 00:17:46.596
And because it is command line interface based, it can also be ran
virtually from anywhere from your local laptop's EC2 instances,

00:17:46.756 --> 00:17:54.676
containers, CI, CD pipelines, or even AWS Batch,
where you can process thousands of repositories in parallel.

00:17:55.676 --> 00:17:58.076
So how does AWS transform custom work?

00:17:58.796 --> 00:18:01.196
Firstly, you can provide a source code.

00:18:01.196 --> 00:18:08.036
So this could be one or more repositories from GitHub,
GitLab, a local machine, or any source control system.

00:18:09.236 --> 00:18:17.716
Next, you can then also provide organization specific instructions
such as custom validation commands, library upgrade requirements,

00:18:17.716 --> 00:18:21.236
or any company specific coding standards or constraints.

00:18:22.036 --> 00:18:24.356
And once you have provided that information,

00:18:24.356 --> 00:18:29.316
AWS Transform custom verifies your code
by building it in the source environment

00:18:29.316 --> 00:18:37.116
and running tests to ensure that everything works as expected
in the original version and one source verification passes.

00:18:37.436 --> 00:18:42.916
AWS Transform custom proceeds
to the transformation phase with its multi-agent system.

00:18:43.436 --> 00:18:46.276
So over here, firstly you have your planner agent.

00:18:46.556 --> 00:18:52.676
The planner agent analyzes the project and generates
a comprehensive step by step transformation plan.

00:18:53.036 --> 00:18:59.676
Human review is invited at this stage to approve,
modify or redirect the plan before execution begins.

00:19:00.796 --> 00:19:06.316
Secondly, your execution agent,
your execution agent, executes the plan incrementally.

00:19:06.516 --> 00:19:12.716
It creates a local staging branch and commits changes to it,
enabling rollback to any point in time.

00:19:13.196 --> 00:19:20.196
And it also provides a live workload so that you can monitor
exactly what is changing now throughout execution.

00:19:20.396 --> 00:19:25.236
The agent references a knowledge base that is built
from the additional context you provide.

00:19:25.236 --> 00:19:31.436
So this could be documentation or organization
specific rules that will be used for code generation.

00:19:32.316 --> 00:19:34.476
And then you have your validation agent.

00:19:34.476 --> 00:19:40.916
Now your validation agent validates the transformation
by ensuring that the transform code compiles successfully.

00:19:41.076 --> 00:19:47.436
So this is done by running unit tests and automating
and executing any automated scripts that may have defined.

00:19:47.836 --> 00:19:54.516
And if validation fails, the self debug agent
automatically takes over to identify and fix those issues.

00:19:55.436 --> 00:19:59.156
Now, after each run, the agent captures knowledge items.

00:19:59.156 --> 00:20:00.000
So knowledge items are automatically extracted.

